{"reference":"1 Kings 12","book":"1 Kings","chapter":12,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why It Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The division of the kingdom is presented on two levels at once: it follows Rehoboam's rejection of experienced counsel and his harsh response to the northern tribes, while also occurring within the judgment previously announced because of Solomon's disloyalty. The chapter therefore does not reduce the rupture to either divine judgment or human political choices alone.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 12:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_012_1de2ad79c71f969c56476223"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Literary Place in 1 Kings","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"This chapter marks a major turn in the movement of 1 Kings. The narrative has moved through contested succession, Solomon's wisdom and temple, and Solomon's failure; here it enters the era of divided kingdoms, a setting that leads into later prophetic confrontation under Ahab.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 12:1-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_012_e9e6b1f1e03f6b1e69adc810"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}